.TH QFW-SERVICES 1 "November 2026" "QFw 0.1.0" "QFw Commands"
.SH NAME
qfw-services \- list the services a runtime profile can start
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B qfw-services
.RI [ --site-config " path" ]
.RI [ --runtime-config " path" | --profile " name" ]
.RI [ --json ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B qfw-services
reads the
.B local-services
manifest of a runtime profile, selected as
.BR qfw-setup (1)
selects it, and prints one record per manifest entry. It starts no service and
needs no run.
.PP
Each record has four fields.
.TP
.B service
The entry's
.BR name .
.TP
.B provider
The
.B provider
property the entry's service module publishes, the one its QPM advertises.
.TP
.B device_id
The entry's
.BR device-id ,
or null.
.TP
.B accelerator
What the module derives from the entry's settings, such as
.B gpu
for an
.B aer
entry with
.BR "device: GPU" ,
or null when the module does not say.
.PP
The command imports each entry's module through
.BR defw-python (1).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI --site-config " path"
Read the install prefix from this site configuration.
.TP
.BI --runtime-config " path"
Read this runtime configuration.
.TP
.BI --profile " name"
Read
.IB name .yaml
under the runtime configuration directory.
.TP
.B --json
Print the records as one JSON list. Without it, each record is one line, with
.B -
for a null field.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero when every entry's module was imported. A configuration without
.B local-services
returns 1, and a module that fails to import returns
.BR defw-python 's
status.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR qfw-setup (1),
.BR qfw-services.yaml (5),
.BR qfw-runtime.yaml (5),
.BR qfw (7)
